  "answer": "Yes, in principle. Under Manual practice, website statistics or other relevant metrics can be used rather than insisting on sales or marketing figures. Examiners must consider the commercial realities of the claimed goods or services. This is not an automatic exemption simply because turnover is low. [TMM/Part23/4/4/1/1]\n\nUseful alternatives include website and social media analytics, funds managed, raised or distributed, and video or podcast viewership figures. For website evidence, the Manual specifically identifies websites where the trade mark is used, supported by figures showing Australian visitors since first use, preferably broken down periodically. [TMM/Part23/4/4/1/1; TMM/Part23/3/3/4~2]\n\nSmall turnover is not assessed in isolation: the Manual says long use may compensate for small turnover, particularly where the mark has some inherent adaptation to distinguish. Turnover must also be considered against the relevant market and unit cost. [TMM/Part23/4/4/1~1]\n\nI cannot say whether your statistics would be sufficient: the Manual requires the strength of the evidence to be balanced against the mark’s inherent adaptation to distinguish. [TMM/Part23/4/4/1~2]\n\nThese statements describe Manual practice, not legislation. No Act or Regulations passages were supplied."
